    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `AcceptanceTest` passing \u2014 it's the only test class in the project).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec: *on successful create, enqueue a welcome notification \u2014 emit via the logger named `NOTIFY` a line containing the owner id and the memberId.*\n\nIn `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner(...)`, right after the owner is saved and the existing `AUDIT` events are emitted, I added a dedicated `NOTIFY` logger and a welcome line:\n\n- Declared `private static final Logger NOTIFY = LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"NOTIFY\");` alongside the existing `AUDIT` logger.\n- Emitted `NOTIFY.info(\"welcome notification enqueued for owner id={} memberId={}\", owner.getId(), owner.getMemberId());` on the successful-create path.\n\nThis mirrors the established audit pattern in the controller. The `memberId` logged comes from `owner.getMemberId()`, which is exactly the value the test reads back via `GET /api/owners/{id}` (the DTO's `memberId` maps straight from it), so `AcceptanceTest.coreEnqueuesWelcome` \u2014 which captures the `NOTIFY` logger and asserts a line contains both the id and memberId \u2014 passes.\n\nThe emission sits after the actual save (and after the per-day / per-city / duplicate rejection checks that return early), so it only fires on a genuinely successful create, not on rejected requests. The idempotent-replay path returns earlier and deliberately does not re-notify.",
